age\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and body and len(body) > self.max_message_chars and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            new_body = self._truncate_message(body)\n            idx = msg.find(body)\n            if idx >= 0:\n                msg = msg[:idx] + new_body + msg[idx+len(body):]\n```\n\nThen multi-line handling uses prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)] — but now body replaced, so record.message not found... Use body variable chosen (the truncated one) for the newline prefix logic: rewrite:\n\n```python\n        if \"\\n\" in msg and body:\n            prefix = msg[: msg.find(body)] if body in msg else \"\"\n            msg = msg.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\n```\n\nHmm original: `if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message:` — it also handles when body empty but exception has newlines: then skip. Keep similar semantics with new body. But if truncated body replaces and body appears in it... `body in msg` fine.\n\nCareful: record.message could be empty string; then skip.\n\nAlso exception text: super().format appends exc text after message. If truncated body replaced before, fine.\n\nNote record.message may be non-str? logging sets message = str(msg). Fine.\n\nAlso if stacked, `msg.find(body)` — if body occurs in the exception text first? body is before exception, so find gets first occurrence — could be in prefix if prefix overlaps. Rare.\n\nTruncate helper:\n\n```python\n    _OMIT_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\n    def _truncate_message(self, message: str) -> str:\n        budget = self.max_message_chars\n        marker = _OMIT_MARKER\n        remaining = budget - len(marker)\n        head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n        tail = remaining - head\n        return message[:head] + marker + (message[len(message) - tail:] if tail else \"\")\n```\n\nCheck example: budget 32, marker len 15 (\"...<omitted>...\" = 3+9+3 = 15), remaining=17, head=9, tail=8. ✓\n\nIf budget >= len(marker)+2 guaranteed since >=32 > 15.\n\nDefault init: `_setup_logger` needs to read `envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS` and pass max_message_chars=... to NewLineFormatter. Import environ: `from deepinfer import envs` — check logger.py imports; currently no. Circular import risk: environ imports os/threading only, so importing `from deepinfer import envs` in logger is fine. But `deepinfer/__init__.py` may import logger... importing `from deepinfer import envs` triggers deepinfer/__init__.py execution. Risk of circular import if deepinfer/__init__ imports logger. Alternative: `from deepinfer.environ import envs` also triggers package __init__. Hmm. In stats_logger they use `from deepinfer import envs`, and logger is imported there. logger is probably imported by deepinfer/__init__? Let me check deepinfer/__init__.py content and how other modules do it. vLLM logger imports `from vllm.envs import VLLM_...`. Actually in vLLM, logger.py doesn't import envs; logging config done at __init__.\n\nSafer: do a lazy import inside `_setup_logger`:\n\n```python\n    from deepinfer import envs  # local import to avoid import cycles\n\n    max_chars = envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS\n```\n\nThat avoids module-import cycle. But does test expect reading at setup time? Yes — cache applies.\n\nHmm, but tests may check that `_setup_logger` reads env var. Lazy import fine.\n\nPossibly tests set env and call `init_logger`? Default init: how is `_setup_logger` invoked? Perhaps `_setup_logger()` at module import and via some function like `init_logger` when `_default_handler is None`? Let me read rest of logger.py.\n\nAlso refresh: `rearm` etc. handled by descriptor.\n\nstats_logger: change `logger.info(m) for m in msgs` to `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nNow read remaining ranges of logger.py and environ.py end.\n\nLet me do read_range for logger.py 88-end, and environ.py 221-end. Actually I need line counts. Use run command? Just read_range with end large; if exceeds, error maybe. Use `pyt